Let $L_{1} \equiv 2 x+y-1=0$
L_{2} \equiv 3 x+2 y-5=0

The equation of straight line passing through the intersection point of the lines $L_{1}$ and $L_{2}$ is given by

\begin{array}{l}
L_{1}+\lambda L_{2}=0 \\
\Rightarrow(2 x+y-1)+\lambda(3 x+2 y-5)=0
\end{array}

Since, this line passes through the origin also

\begin{array}{l}
(0+0-1)+\lambda(0+0-5)=0 \\
\Rightarrow-5 \lambda=1 \Rightarrow \lambda=-\frac{1}{5}
\end{array}

Required line is

\begin{array}{l}
(2 x+y-1)-\frac{1}{5}(3 x+2 y-5)=0 \\
\Rightarrow\left(2-\frac{3}{5}\right) x+\left(1-\frac{2}{5}\right) y-1+1=0 \\
\Rightarrow \frac{7}{5} x+\frac{3}{5} y=0 \Rightarrow 7 x+3 y=0
\end{array}

A and B will agree in a certain statement if both speak truth or both tell a lie. We define following events

\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{E}_{1}=\mathrm{A} \text { and } \mathrm{B} \text { both speak truth } \Rightarrow \mathrm{P}\left(\mathrm{E}_{1}\right)=\mathrm{xy} \\
\mathrm{E}_{2}=\mathrm{A} \text { and } \mathrm{B} \text { both tell a lie } \\
\Rightarrow \mathrm{P}\left(\mathrm{E}_{2}\right)=(1-\mathrm{x})(1-\mathrm{y}) \\
\mathrm{E}=\mathrm{A} \text { and } \mathrm{B} \text { agree in a certain statement }
\end{array}

Clearly, $\mathrm{P}\left(\mathrm{E} / \mathrm{E}_{1}\right)=1$ and $\mathrm{P}\left(\mathrm{E} / \mathrm{E}_{2}\right)=1$
The required probability is $\mathrm{P}\left(\mathrm{E}_{1} / \mathrm{E}\right)$.
Using Baye's theorem

\begin{array}{l}
P\left(E_{1} / E\right) \\
=\frac{P\left(E_{1}\right) P\left(E / E_{1}\right)}{P\left(E_{1}\right) P\left(E / E_{1}\right)+P\left(E_{2}\right) P\left(E / E_{2}\right)} \\
=\frac{x y .1}{x y .1+(1-x)(1-y) \cdot 1}=\frac{x y}{1-x-y+2 x y}
\end{array}
